                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Most Bans Are Preventable</h2>
<p>Ask any出海 ad manager why their account got banned, and you'll likely hear vague answers like "platform decision," "creative violation," or "triggered a review." But dig deeper, and you'll find that <strong>80% of account suspensions aren't sudden—they're the result of accumulated risks that went unchecked</strong>. The root cause is almost always the same: no systematic compliance review SOP in place.</p>
<p>This article isn't about platform policy jargon. Instead, it focuses on the operational level: <strong>Which review actions can catch risks early and prevent account bans?</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Three High-Risk Scenarios: Where Problems Usually Appear</h2>
<h3>1. Account Level: Legacy Issues with Credentials and Permissions</h3>
<p>When team members leave, admin access often goes unrevoked. Business licenses change but ad account verification doesn't get updated. Or the attribution chain between parent and sub-accounts becomes messy. These are the most commonly overlooked daily operational issues.</p>
<p><strong>Review Actions (Monthly):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Audit all account admin roles and confirm active employment status</li>
<li>Verify that business license and legal representative info match the account entity</li>
<li>Review access permissions for Pixels and Business Manager (BM) accounts</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>A utility app once had an ex-employee accidentally trigger an ad complaint because their access wasn't revoked after leaving. The account entered review status, disrupting campaigns for two weeks. This kind of incident is more common than you'd think in larger跨境 teams.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>2. Creative Level: The "Gradual Decay" of Compliant Copy</h3>
<p>An ad creative might be compliant when it launches, but as platform policies evolve and sensitivity around certain terms shifts, previously safe creatives can "expire." For example, if "lowest price" or "#1 in the world" suddenly become flagged terms, outdated creatives become liabilities.</p>
<p><strong>Review Actions (Weekly):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Scan active creatives against the latest platform policy checklist</li>
<li>Verify landing page consistency with ad copy (no exaggerated claims)</li>
<li>Check whether creatives targeting sensitive regions include required compliance disclaimers</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Funding and Reconciliation: Anomalies in the Money Flow</h3>
<p>When recharge amounts don't match actual account spending, or when "recharge successful" notifications aren't reflected in the balance, it often signals payment-side risks. Once an account gets flagged for payment issues, the funding freeze can be devastating to campaign schedules.</p>
<p><strong>Review Actions (Daily/Weekly):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Cross-check recharge records against platform backend balance</li>
<li>Monitor account balance thresholds to avoid mid-campaign pauses</li>
<li>Regularly export spend reports to identify anomalies (e.g., a single ad group spiking 300% in daily spend)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>The Compliance Review SOP: A Simplified Execution Checklist</h2>
<p>Given that most运营 teams are already stretched thin, here's a <strong>minimum viable</strong> checklist—focused on core actions per cycle rather than exhaustive coverage:</p>
<p><strong>Daily Review (5 minutes)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Check account status: any unresolved policy warnings?</li>
<li>Confirm today's recharge has cleared</li>
<li>Verify active campaigns are running normally</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Weekly Review (20 minutes)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Summarize this week's creative review feedback, flag problematic assets</li>
<li>Compare this week's spend report against expectations, flag anomalies</li>
<li>Update local policy change log if applicable</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Monthly Review (1 hour)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Full audit of account permissions and admin list</li>
<li>Check credential certification expiration dates</li>
<li>Debrief any bans or warnings this month, update preventive checklist</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>A Supplementary Principle: Don't Let Compliance Kill Efficiency</h2>
<p>Much of the resistance to compliance reviews comes not from unwillingness, but from the feeling that it's too time-consuming. In practice: <strong>embedding review actions into your daily workflow is far more efficient than setting aside dedicated hours for compliance.</strong></p>
<p>For example, spend 2 minutes before logging off checking account status, add a "compliance risks" row to your weekly report, or set up a simple review template in Notion or Feishu. These small, consistent actions compound far better than firefighting after a problem surfaces.</p>
<p>Compliance isn't a one-time task—it's ongoing risk management. Turn it into a SOP, and account stability will follow.</p>
